# DRAFT - A dot in the source path (not spaces) triggers joblib's jupyter false failure

id:                     DRAFT-joblib-1.6.0-dot-in-path-jupyter-false-failure
trigger_tool:           pytest|joblib|inspect.getsourcefile
phase:                  test
error_signature:        assert "ipython-input" in f_cache_relative_directory|test_memory.py:191: AssertionError|test_parallel_call_cached_function_defined_in_jupyter

severity:               2
category:               ohos, conan, python, testing, environment, false-positive
match_keywords:         joblib, pytest, false failure, dot in path, .cache, conan cache, inspect.getsourcefile, name mangling, test_memory, jupyter, ipython-input, allow-list, cwd, abspath
title:                  False pytest failure caused by a dot in the source path, misdiagnosed as spaces

root_cause:             joblib's func_inspect.get_func_name, for module == "__main__", takes os.path.abspath(inspect.getsourcefile(func)), splits it on os.sep, joins the parts with "-" into the module prefix, and only THEN does module.split("."). Because the join happens before the split, any DOT in the path is treated as a segment separator, so the mangled cache directory name is truncated at the first dot and no longer contains "<ipython-input". The upstream test test_parallel_call_cached_function_defined_in_jupyter therefore fails. This was initially and incorrectly attributed to spaces in the path; a clean path WITHOUT spaces still fails, while a path WITH spaces but no dot passes.
affected_scope:         Any upstream suite that derives names or hashes from an absolute source path (joblib, and generally libraries using inspect.getsourcefile()/__file__), when the path contains a dot directory (.cache, ~/.local, package dirs like foo-1.6.0) — including the CI worker path /storage/Users/currentUser/.cache/buildbot-worker/conan-pr-check/build, so it DOES reproduce on CI.
verification_cmd:       B=/tmp/p; mkdir -p "$B/nospace/dot.1.6.0" "$B/cleanpath" "$B/with space nodot"; cp -r <src>/{joblib,conftest.py} "$B/nospace/dot.1.6.0/"; cd "$B/nospace/dot.1.6.0" && PYTHONPATH=$PWD python3 -m pytest "<case>"   # 2 failed;  cd "$B/cleanpath" -> 2 passed

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           run-with-a-dot-free-cwd
rec_description:        Run the test process with a cwd that contains no dot. inspect.getsourcefile() resolves through os.path.abspath(), so the mangled name is derived from the CWD, not from where the source tree lives: a dot-free cwd plus PYTHONPATH pointing at the dot-containing source tree passes (verified). Do NOT change the recipe and do NOT add the case to ALLOWED_TEST_FAILURES. If the case must be explained, document the four-way experiment (space-no-dot passes, dot-no-space fails, .cache-style dot dir fails, dot-free passes).
rec_command:            cd <dot-free-dir> && PYTHONPATH=<src-with-dots> python3 -m pytest "<case>"
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               manual

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      relocate-the-build-tree
fallback_description:   If a dot-free cwd is impractical, copy/patch the source tree under a path with no dots at all (neither the cwd nor any parent component). This makes abspath() dot-free and the direct run passes.
fallback_command:
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          manual

forbid_1_pattern:       ALLOWED_TEST_FAILURES.*jupyter|allow.?list.*dot.*path|space-in-path.*root.?cause
forbid_1_reason:        Allow-listing an environment-induced false failure freezes a non-issue into "known failure" and can mask a genuine regression later (R51: do not paper over). Attributing it to "spaces" is also wrong and leads to the wrong workaround.
forbid_1_consequence:   A real future breakage of the same test is silently accepted; meanwhile the actual trigger (dots) is left in the CI path and the failure keeps recurring.
forbid_2_pattern:       patch.*joblib.*func_inspect|modify.*upstream.*test.*to.*pass
forbid_2_reason:        The defect is in the local path shape, not in the package; patching upstream code or tests to make a path-induced failure disappear is not a fix.
forbid_2_consequence:   Divergence from upstream for no functional reason; the "fix" is invalidated as soon as the path changes.
forbid_3_pattern:       -p no:logging.*pytest
forbid_3_reason:        -p no:logging disables the plugin that provides the caplog fixture, producing a spurious "fixture 'caplog' not found" error unrelated to the package.
forbid_3_consequence:   A false ERROR is reported and the pass count is understated (1325 instead of 1326).

principles:             P010,P014
causes:                 []
caused_by:              []
